WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.310
Someone in the meeting just asked a question.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.210
What happens when someone places an order?

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:13.150
Silence. So you draw it. A request hits a service. The service writes it down. Easy!

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:19.435
This is Draft Canvas. A diagramming tool for people who build software, and then have to explain it.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.359
Call it a topic, and boom! The arrow becomes an event.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.010
Add a queue, and it is already suggesting the worker. It knows. It always knows.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:38.210
Tell it Postgres, Stripe, Kafka. It knows exactly what each one is, logo and all.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.630
Now stop drawing, and follow it.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.470
One order, from the very first request,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.290
all the way to the last notification.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:59.190
Want that as a sequence diagram? One command. Done.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:06.950
Crack open a service, and look inside. C4 levels, all the way down.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:14.570
Take the email provider down. Go on! See what breaks, and what shrugs it off.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:19.510
And where your drawing has no answer, it asks. Rude! But fair.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:26.003
Or hand it to your own AI agent, right from the terminal. It reads the diagram, and fixes it.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.772
And bring the whole meeting in. One link. No accounts.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:41.190
New in Draft Canvas 1.22: a style of your own, saved in the diagram.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:49.416
A linked junction is one point drawn in two places. Press G, and you're there.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:56.350
And just like that, the system that lived in five different heads is one picture everyone can read.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.682
Draft Canvas. For meetings that suddenly need a diagram.

